[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Core Concepts & Architecture in GraphQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"GraphQL is a query language for APIs and a runtime for fulfilling those queries with your existing data. It provides a complete and understandable description of the data in your API, gives clients the power to ask for exactly what they need and nothing more, makes it easier to evolve APIs over time, and enables powerful developer tools."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The GraphQL Architecture Overview"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike traditional REST architecture, which relies on multiple endpoints representing different resources, a GraphQL architecture typically consists of a single endpoint. The client sends a query detailing the exact shape of the data it requires, and the GraphQL server processes, validates, and resolves the request against the backend data sources."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Single Endpoint: All requests flow through a single URL, usually /graphql, avoiding the over-fetching and under-fetching problems of REST."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Strongly Typed Schema: The schema defines the contract between the client and the server, specifying what queries, mutations, and subscriptions are available."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Resolvers: Functions responsible for populating the data for each field in your schema by fetching data from databases, microservices, or third-party APIs."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining the Schema"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The GraphQL Schema Definition Language (SDL) is used to define types, queries, and mutations. Here is an example of a simple User type and a query to fetch a user by ID."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"type User {
  id: ID!
  name: String!
  email: String!
  posts: [Post!]!
}

type Post {
  id: ID!
  title: String!
  content: String!
}

type Query {
  user(id: ID!): User
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Resolvers"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"A resolver is a collection of functions that generate values for a GraphQL type. Every field in a GraphQL schema is backed by a resolver function. If you do not provide a custom resolver, GraphQL provides a default one that looks for a property matching the field name on the parent object."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const resolvers = {
  Query: {
    user: async (parent, args, context, info) => {
      return await context.db.findUserById(args.id);
    },
  },
  User: {
    posts: async (parent, args, context, info) => {
      return await context.db.findPostsByUserId(parent.id);
    },
  },
};"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Executing Operations"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Clients interact with the GraphQL server using three primary operation types: Queries (fetching data), Mutations (modifying data), and Subscriptions (listening to real-time events via WebSockets). Below is an example of a client query."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"query GetUserProfile {
  user(id: "123") {
    name
    email
    posts {
      title
    }
  }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By mastering these core concepts and understanding the underlying architecture, you can design highly efficient, flexible, and scalable APIs using GraphQL."}]
